<article class="env-lemma" id="07V1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07V1">Lemma <span data-tag="07V1">69.16.4</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/07UY" data-tag="07UY">69.16.1</a>. Let $Z_0, Z_1 \subset |X|$ be disjoint closed subsets. Then there exists an $a \in A$ such that $Z_0 \subset V(a)$ and $Z_1 \subset V(a - 1)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We may and do endow $Z_0$, $Z_1$ with the reduced induced subspace structure (Properties of Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/047X" data-tag="047X">66.12.5</a>) and we denote $i_0 : Z_0 \to X$ and $i_1 : Z_1 \to X$ the corresponding closed immersions. Since $Z_0 \cap Z_1 = \emptyset $ we see that the canonical map of quasi-coherent $\mathcal{O}_ X$-modules 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathcal{O}_ X \longrightarrow i_{0, *}\mathcal{O}_{Z_0} \oplus i_{1, *}\mathcal{O}_{Z_1}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is surjective (look at stalks at geometric points). Since $H^1(X, -)$ is zero on the kernel of this map the induced map of global sections is surjective. Thus we can find $a \in A$ which maps to the global section $(0, 1)$ of the right hand side. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>